 "title": "Which server gets the request?",
 "sources": [
  "nginx docs, 'Using nginx as HTTP load balancer' (http://nginx.org/en/docs/http/load_balancing.html, fetched 2026-11-24): 'When the load balancing method is not specifically configured, it defaults to round-robin'; 'least-connected — next request is assigned to the server with the least number of active connections'; 'Least-connected allows controlling the load on application instances more fairly in a situation when some of the requests take longer to complete'; passive health checks: on an error nginx 'will mark this server as failed, and will try to avoid selecting this server for subsequent inbound requests for a while'.",
  "Illustration only: servers A and B and their request counts are an example."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Server A is busy with slow requests, and server B is idle. With round robin, where does the next request go? Guess.",
   "caps": "A IS *BUSY | B IS *IDLE | WHERE *NEXT?"
  },
  {
   "text": "It can go to A. Round robin just takes turns, it doesn't look at load.",
   "caps": "CAN BE *A | JUST TAKES *TURNS | IGNORES *LOAD"
  },
  {
   "text": "It's the nginx default, and it's fine when every request costs about the same.",
   "caps": "NGINX *DEFAULT | FINE IF *EQUAL"
  },
  {
   "text": "Least connections fixes it: the next request goes to the server with the fewest active connections.",
   "caps": "*LEAST CONNECTIONS | FEWEST *ACTIVE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So B gets it, and the busy server can catch up.",
   "caps": "*B GETS IT | A *CATCHES UP"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: neither one helps if a server is dead.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | A *DEAD SERVER"
  },
  {
   "text": "Nginx marks a server as failed when its requests error, and skips it for a while.",
   "caps": "MARKED *FAILED | *SKIPPED | FOR A *WHILE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So use least connections when requests vary, and keep health checks on. Did you pick A?",
   "caps": "*LEAST CONNECTIONS | HEALTH *CHECKS | DID YOU PICK *A?"
  }
 ],
